My apologies to readers for my previous flames.  I will control myself
this time.  Really.

In article <1990Jun4.064712.18674@msuinfo.cl.msu.edu> Terry Conkin writes:
>We'll even go so far as to make purists happy and say default to OFF.
>

This is a convenient compromise.  I think that I could live with it.
But it would be interesting to see the complaints from new players a
year from now.  My imagination: "So the default for pet abuse is OFF
but the default for suicide is ON?  That doesn't make sense!"

>I hope none of the purists (wonder if they really play !confirm, hmm?)
>are implying that everyone MUST play their way?  [reference to SS uniforms
> deleted]

I have been arguing with the purists for a while and I can assure you
that they are not nazis.  David and others have made a valid argument,
which I agree with, that the game should "keep people on their toes."
Beginning and intermediate players stay on their toes because the
monsters are so nasty.  Really high-level characters (read 21st level
with AC -17, +4 Firebrand, instrinsic resist everything, teleport
ability, 250HP, and maybe a few things I should not mention) can
become very complacent, because they can beat the tar out of any
monster in the dungeon.  If they get really unlucky in battle, they
can usually teleport away and recover.  The possibility of sudden
death adds an element of realistic fear to the game.

>Was it like this when 'confirm' was first added as an option too?
>

I wasn't reading rgh then but I imagine not.  I think the "confirm"
feature was bundled with alignments.  It would be very tedious to use
the / command before each attack in order to test alignment.
Preventing attacks on your pet and shopkeepers is, I think, a happy but
unintentional side-effect.  Although I would like pool-confirm and
monster-confirm, I think that the current confirm option is logically
consistent with or without them.

There seem to be two conflicting desires here:

(1) To make the game more forgiving of obvious blunders that could be
caused by mistyping.

(2) To make the game hard and scary so that players will keep on their
toes.

Solutions proposed to (1):

    (a) add noblunder-type options
    (b) flush typeahead in certain situations

Obviously I prefer (a).  I think that (b) would be helpful in many
situations.  Rogue flushed typeahead in battle.  This saved my
rogueskin on numerous occasions.  I don't know if the flush applied to
the first hit though, since you weren't yet in battle; the first hit
is the deadly one with floating eyes.  And what about pools?  Should
we flush typeahead whenever the player is next to water?  I am not
sure I would like that.  Also, I doubt that flushing typeahead would
work well over a modem or telnet connection, since the typeahead might
be at the other end of the line; this is the most serious defect, I
think, in option (b).  A flushinbattle option would be quite helpful
as a supplement to noblunder-type options, though, IMHO.

Solutions to (2):

    (a) don't add noblunder option -- this keeps the game scary
    (b) make the game harder in other ways

I think that (b) would be more fun for everyone -- even if the
noblunder-type options are not added.  Let's give players something
*really* to be scared about.  You've heard some of my ideas: slippery
rocks next to pools, moat monsters that can pull you under, ogres that
can push you in.  Then even careful players could enjoy abject
terror.  How about letting floating eyes attack you from a distance?
I have never understood why they only attack you when you swing at
them.  If I stand right next to an eye and throw darts at it, it has
no counterattack.  Why not?  I'm looking at it and I'm within its
range.  If I was several squares away I can imagine that its gaze
would be less effective, but I still have to look at it in order to
throw missile weapons.  If this is changed, then game balance would
have to be carefully considered on the low levels.

My opinion: allow confirmation of blunders and make the game harder in
other ways.

>Meanwhile, to start something totally different - some people
>misunderstood my bitch about eating. NO, I WAS NOT SATIATED! I was 
>"normal" - and received no warning and choked, presto! Huh?
>

You didn't really deserve to get flamed.  They probably just didn't
believe you.  If I eat when not satiated I can't recall ever choking.
The code in pl7 at least checks for this explicitly.  Perhaps you
encountered a bug.  Which patchlevel?

Minor question: I would be interested in knowing the rationale for
the safepet option.  This seems to protect only against mistyping.  Is
it because there are so many d's running around that it would be
tedious to check which is your dog?  The same is true, to a much
lesser extent of e's, since the same letter is used for freezing
spheres.  (Side note: I think the game plays much better with confirm
ON and safepet OFF.  You still don't hit your pet and you don't waste
a move.)

